  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1234 PM CDT WED SEP 23 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery late this morning reveals a trough swinging\nacross the Tennessee Valley as an upper level ridge builds out to\nour west. At the surface, a cold front can be seen draped across\nCentral Alabama. As we head through the day, a mid-level vort max\nwill rotate around the base of the upper trough. This will\ntrigger another round of isolated to scattered convection this\nafternoon and evening across our northeastern areas. Increased\ncloud cover this morning will help limit our instability through\nthe day. However, forecast soundings continue to hint at around\n1000 J/kg CAPE and a profile supportive of damaging wind gusts.\nConvection will wane through the evening hours as our upper level\nsupport peels off to the northeast and drier air begins filtering\nacross the region. We continue with low chances for showers and\nstorms on Thursday as moisture wraps around the backside of the\ntrough. Most locations are likely to remain dry tomorrow. Upper\nlevel ridging then takes control of the region, leaving us dry\nthrough the weekend.\n\nThis afternoon will be our warmest day of the week with highs\nsettling into the upper 80s to mid 90s. A much needed cool down\nis in store for the rest of the week as high temperatures top out\nin the upper 70s to mid 80s. The most noticeable change will be\nour overnight temps with lows dropping into the upper 50s to mid\n60s by the end of the work week.\n\n95/Castillo",
